### 2026-08-29T03:53:59.718Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

I've generalized this. Rather than one-off tests pinned to the exact reported viewports, I added three mechanism-agnostic checks into the existing full-matrix exploratory test (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`), which already sweeps every mode × drawer-state × 5 random screen sizes across a 320–1920 × 480–1080 range:

1. **`drawerClipping`** — no visible content inside an expanded `#top-drawer` should be centered outside the drawer's own horizontal bounds (catches "content silently wrapped/pushed off-screen and clipped" — the "hidden rules" bug, at any width/mode).
2. **`controlsDominance`** — a mode's own `#<mode>-controls` panel shouldn't fill more than 75% of the viewport height on mobile/tablet, regardless of *why* it stretched (catches "the floating-panel treatment fell back to normal flow" — the giant-blank-spot bug's actual root shape).
3. **`floatingPanelGap`** — a floating overlay panel (`#sidebar`, `#blast-stats`, etc.) shouldn't leave much blank space below its real content (catches the older "floats correctly but renders at a fixed cap regardless of content" variant of the same symptom).

I validated all three against both real bugs by disabling each fix in turn and confirming the checks go red, then confirming green with the fix restored. Let me kick off the full matrix run now to make sure nothing else in that wide sweep trips these new checks.


