Yes. A bass clef containing only rests is likely to be read as an additional task rather than as useful information, especially for a [REDACTED_STUDENT].

A good rule would be:

- If the entire selected song or practice phrase has no notes assigned to one clef, render only the active clef.
- If both clefs contain music anywhere in the selected material, retain both staves for stable orientation.
- Do not dynamically hide/show a clef halfway through a phrase; that would make the page jump.
- Base the decision on the rendered staff assignment, not simply on MIDI pitch, so it remains correct after notation or hand-selection choices.

For a high melody-only song, the learner would see a treble staff without a distracting bass staff full of rests. For a genuine grand-staff piece, both remain visible, including necessary rests.

This also fits the broader texture choices: “melody-only” could naturally produce a single staff, while “full texture” would use the grand staff when both registers are present.


