### 2026-08-30T15:59:41.510Z | codex | codex | conversation 01a04e07-94d6-7832-9230-353fae598034

Yes, you recall correctly: [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]

The current mechanism is a sliding notation window:

- At the first guidance position, it shows up to three previous notes, the current note, and three upcoming notes.
- At the reduced-guidance positions, it shows up to three previous notes plus the current note.
- As the current index changes, the window is rebuilt and the visible notes shift.
- During guide playback, the current index is temporarily overridden for each played note, so the staff can shift once per guide note.
- When the sequence is completed, Random generates a new sequence rather than presenting a finite-song ending.

That is the likely source of the jumpiness. [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] The current target can therefore move, notes can disappear from one edge and appear at the other, and the layout can be recalculated while the guide is playing.

This was originally intended to make Random a memory quiz rather than a readable song. But pedagogically it creates an ambiguity:

> Is the child supposed to remember the whole invented phrase, or only chase the currently visible moving window?

For a young learner, I think the staff should remain spatially stable during one attempt. Possible fixes include:

- Render the whole generated Random sequence, but dim or blur non-current material.
- Render a fixed-size window whose geometry stays constant as its contents change.
- Keep the guide’s current-note highlight moving without rebuilding the staff.
- Avoid resetting the visible window to the beginning after each prompt.
- Treat Random as a short phrase exercise rather than an endlessly shifting quiz.

The replay’s concentrated searching and later frustration make the current sliding-window behavior especially suspect: the child may have been trying to understand a moving visual target rather than simply failing to find notes.


